.TH PKG 1
.SH NAME
pkg \- add packages to the name space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pkg add
[
.B -f
.I file
] [
.I pkg ...
]
.br
.B pkg run
[
.B -f
.I file
] [
.I pkg ...
] [
.B -c
.I cmd
[
.I arg ...
]
]
.br
.B pkg list
.br
.B pkg pin
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A package is a tree of files in the shape of the root:
.BR bin ,
.BR sys/lib ,
and so on.
It is not copied anywhere to be installed: adding it binds its directories into the name space,
so it is there for the process that adds it and those it starts, and for no others.
A window can have one version of a package and the next window another.
.PP
The system's packages are in
.BI /pkg/ name / hash\f1,
one tree for each version of each package.
A tree never changes:
.I hash
is a hash of its files, so another version is another tree, and both stay.
.B /pkg/index
names each package's current tree, a line each:
.IP
.EX
pkg=git hash=3f2a9c0d1e2b4a6f
.EE
.PP
Each tree holds, beside its files:
.TF namespace
.TP
.B namespace
The package's binds, in the form of
.IR namespace (6),
with
.B $pkg
its tree:
.B "bind -a $pkg/bin /bin"
for git.
A union is of one directory, not of the directories in it,
so a package binds onto the directories it adds to, not their parents.
.TP
.B meta
In the form of
.IR ndb (6):
the package's name,
.BR pkg= ,
and the packages it needs,
.BR need= ,
a pair each.
.TP
.B proto
The tree's files, in the form of
.IR proto (2).
.PD
.PP
A
.I pkg
is one of
.TF name@hash
.TP
.I name
The tree
.B /pkg/index
names.
.TP
.IB name @ hash
That tree of
.BR /pkg .
.TP
.I path
A tree of one's own, a path that starts with
.BR / ,
.BR ./ ,
or
.BR ../ :
a package one has built, as it is.
.PD
.PP
.I Pkg add
adds each
.I pkg
to the name space, after the packages it needs, each once:
one in the name space already is not added again.
With
.BI -f " file\f1,
it adds the packages
.I file
names, a line each: a name; a name, a blank, and a hash; or a path.
Lines that start with
.B #
are comments.
The profile that
.IR newuser (8)
makes adds those of
.BR $home/lib/pkg ,
which starts with
.BR git .
.PP
.I Pkg run
adds the packages in a new name space, and runs
.I cmd
there, or else an interactive
.IR rc (1);
the name space it was run in is as it was.
.PP
.I Pkg list
prints the packages of
.B /pkg
in the name space, a line each: a name and a hash.
.PP
.I Pkg pin
rewrites
.IR file ,
by default
.BR $home/lib/pkg ,
with each package that is a name alone given the hash of its current tree,
so that adding the file's packages gives those trees and no later ones.
A copy of the file kept before is a way back.
.SH EXAMPLES
Try a package one has built before it is published:
.IP
.EX
pkg run ./git -c git/log
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF $home/lib/pkg
.TP
.B /pkg
The system's packages.
.TP
.B $home/lib/pkg
The packages the profile adds.
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B rc/bin/pkg
and
.BR mk/pkg ;
the packages' own files are in its
.BI pkg/ name\f1.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR namespace (4),
.IR namespace (6),
.IR proto (2)
.SH BUGS
The packages are only those the site was built with, fetched as they are read;
there is not yet a way to fetch others, or to keep them for when the site cannot be reached.
